   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### Result: a **one-parameter** family, not two; and the fundamental-site bracket alone is not a rotation\n\n**Exact pattern.** The per-site rotation covector $(e_8e^{c_1E_{32}})\\otimes(e_3e^{c_2E_{32}})$ has SoV\nsupport on exactly the four $x_{11}$ labels (support $\\{20,21,23,24\\}$), with\n\n$$c \\;=\\; \\begin{pmatrix}1 & \\frac{47}{28}c_2\\\\[2pt] c_1-\\frac{19}{28}c_2 & c_1c_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\n\\det c \\;=\\; -\\frac{19\\,c_2\\,(28c_1-47c_2)}{784},\\qquad \\tfrac{47}{28}=\\tfrac{t-2h}{t-h}.$$\n\nA product of two one-site brackets has a **rank-one** coefficient matrix $c_{ij}=r_1^{i-1}r_2^{j-1}$, so it\nis a per-site rotation exactly on the two branches of $\\det c=0$:\n\n| branch | weights | rotation |\n|---|---|---|\n| $c_2=0$ | $r_1=c_1$, $r_2=0$ | $e^{c_1E_{32}}\\otimes 1$, $c_1$ free (this is §8) |\n| $c_1=\\frac{47}{28}c_2$ | $r_1=c_2$, $r_2=c_1$ | $e^{c_1E_{32}}\\otimes e^{c_2E_{32}}$ with $c_1/c_2=\\frac{t-2h}{t-h}$ **fixed** |\n\nSo the conjecture is **false as stated**: the product of two one-site brackets never gives independent\n$c_1,c_2$. It gives a one-parameter family whose ratio is fixed by $\\theta$ (a pole ratio, as always in\nthis notebook). Off the locus the object is not even a product covector.\n\n**Realisation.** A two-pole measure $1/\\big((u-p_{s,1})(u-p_{s,2})\\big)$ dressed by $e^{k_su}$ gives\n$r_s=-e^{k_sh}$, so the locus is\n\n$$k_2-k_1 \\;=\\; \\frac{1}{h}\\log\\frac{t-2h}{t-h} \\;=\\; \\log\\frac{47}{28}\\;\\approx\\;0.5178,\\qquad (c_1,c_2)=(-e^{k_2h},\\,-e^{k_1h}).$$\n\nChecked at $k_1=0$ (giving $(c_1,c_2)=(-\\frac{47}{28},-1)$) and $k_1=\\frac12$ (giving\n$(-\\frac{47}{28}e^{1/2},-e^{1/2})$), residuals $\\sim10^{-94}$, twist-independent (rank $\\{c(z),c(z')\\}=1$),\n$\\sigma_2/\\sigma_1\\sim10^{-96}$ for the product test.\n\n**Two negatives worth recording.**\n\n1. **Equal dressings** $k_1=k_2$ — and in particular the natural residue ratios of $1/(\\nu_1\\nu_2)$,\n   $\\{r_1,r_2\\}=\\{-\\frac{9}{47},-\\frac{47}{9}\\}$ — give an object that is neither a product nor a rotation.\n   With those *same* weights the $2\\times2$ **determinant** of §13–§14 is the global $e^{cE_{32}}$. Product\n   versus determinant of the same two brackets are genuinely different covectors, and only the\n   determinant reaches the global rotation.\n2. **The one-site bracket on the fundamental site** ($r_1=0$, $r_2\\neq0$) is **not** a rotation of any\n   kind, and not a product ($\\sigma_2/\\sigma_1=1.9\\times10^{-2}$, global residual $4.8\\times10^{-2}$). Its\n   pattern $\\begin{pmatrix}1&r_2\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$ needs $c_1-\\frac{19}{28}c_2=0$ together with\n   $c_1c_2=0$, i.e. $c_2=0$. The two sites are **not** interchangeable here: the adjoint site's $x_{11}$\n   shift alone is a rotation, the fundamental site's alone is not.\n\n*Trap (again).* For the non-product rows the per-site slice fits still return residuals $\\sim10^{-95}$;\nthey are meaningless until $\\sigma_2/\\sigma_1\\approx0$. The verdict column applies the product test first.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## 17. Wronskian minors: the $E_{21}$ / $E_{31}$ directions\n\nOpen item 1 of the README. A $q_1$-only object can only move the $x_{11}$ coordinates, which is why\neverything built so far landed on $E_{32}$ (or on the surface $b=a(c-a)$). Moving $x_{21}$, $x_{22}$\nneeds $q_2$ as well: a minor with $i=1$ **and** $i=2$ rows, whose determinant produces the Wronskian\n$W(x_{21},x_{22})=Q_1(x_{21})Q_2(x_{22})-Q_2(x_{21})Q_1(x_{22})$ out of which $\\Psi_A(x)$ is built.\n\nFirst a bookkeeping step: *which SoV labels does each generator actually need?* The site-1 coordinates\nare $x_{11}=\\theta_1+h\\lambda_{21}$ with $\\lambda_{21}\\in\\{1,2\\}$, $x_{21}=\\theta_1+h\\lambda_{11}$ and\n$x_{22}=\\theta_1+h(\\lambda_{22}-1)$, subject to $\\lambda_{22}\\le\\lambda_{11}\\le\\lambda_{21}$ and\n$\\lambda_{22}\\le1$; the base label $x_0$ has $(\\lambda_{21},\\lambda_{11},\\lambda_{22})=(1,0,0)$ on site 1\nand $(0,0,0)$ on site 2.\n\nThe structural facts about what a bracket object can produce:\n\n- A $2\\times2$ minor with one $Q_1$ row and one $Q_2$ row, columns giving the shifts $\\sigma=0,-h$,\n  equals $\\sum_{p,p'}v_pv'_{p'}\\,W(p+\\sigma_1,\\,p'+\\sigma_2)$ — the diagonal terms cancel by\n  antisymmetry, so it is automatically a sum of Wronskians. With the site-2 poles cancelled and only\n  $p\\in\\{\\theta_1,\\theta_1+h\\}$ surviving it reaches $x_{21}\\in\\{\\theta_1,\\theta_1+h\\}$,\n  $x_{22}\\in\\{\\theta_1-h,\\theta_1\\}$, i.e. the **three** labels with $x_{11}$ unmoved.\n- Keeping the pole $\\theta_1+2h$ as well would produce $W(\\theta_1+2h,\\cdot)$, i.e. $\\lambda_{11}=2$,\n  which is not a legal label unless $x_{11}$ also moves to $\\theta_1+2h$ — so that needs a $Q_1$ row\n  supplying the $Q_1(x_{11})$ factor too, i.e. a $3\\times3$ minor with rows $(1,1,2)$.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* ---- 21. Which SoV labels does each generator need? The reach of a site-1-only object. ---- *)\nClearAll[s1RotV, locC, locSupport, genTab1, site1Block, threeLab, eqE, eq3, eqG8, eqG3, gbTab];\ns1RotV[a_, b_, c_] := Flatten[Outer[Times,\n    e8low . nilExpT2[JJ[2, 1, λ1], a, d1] . nilExpT2[JJ[3, 1, λ1], b, d1] . nilExpT2[JJ[3, 2, λ1], c, d1], e3low]];\nlocC = Expand[s1RotV[aa, bb, cc] . XNinv];\nlocSupport = Flatten @ Position[locC, _?(# =!= 0 &), {1}, Heads -> False];\ngenTab1 = Flatten[Table[With[{c1 = locC /. Thread[DeleteCases[{aa, bb, cc}, var] -> 0]},\n     Table[{var, labelOffset[patPairs[[x]]], Factor[c1[[x]]]}, {x, Flatten @ Position[c1, _?(# =!= 0 &), {1}, Heads -> False]}]],\n    {var, {aa, bb, cc}}], 1];\n(* labels with site 2 at its base value: exactly what an object built from site-1 Q-values can touch *)\nsite1Block = Select[Range[dtot], labelOffset[patPairs[[#]]][[2]] === {0, 0, 0} &];\n(* the three labels a 2x2 Wronskian minor over the poles {θ1, θ1+h} reaches (x11 unmoved) *)\nthreeLab = Table[SelectFirst[Range[dtot], labelOffset[patPairs[[#]]] === {o, {0, 0, 0}} &], {o, {{0, 0, 0}, {0, 1, 0}, {0, 1, 1}}}];\n(* \"supported inside a label set\" = the coefficients outside it vanish. Groebner basis of that ideal;\n   Solve is hopeless on these systems, GroebnerBasis is instant. *)\neqE = DeleteCases[Factor /@ locC[[Complement[Range[dtot], site1Block]]], 0];\neq3 = DeleteCases[Factor /@ locC[[Complement[Range[dtot], threeLab]]], 0];\neqG8 = DeleteCases[Factor /@ cRotSym[[Complement[Range[dtot], site1Block]]], 0];\neqG3 = DeleteCases[Factor /@ cRotSym[[Complement[Range[dtot], threeLab]]], 0];\ngbTab = {\n   {\"site-1-local rotation, support in the 8 site-2-base labels\", eqE, GroebnerBasis[eqE, {aa, bb, cc}], \"a = b = 0, c free: E32 only\"},\n   {\"site-1-local rotation, support in the 3 Wronskian labels\", eq3, GroebnerBasis[eq3, {aa, bb, cc}], \"a = b = c = 0: nothing\"},\n   {\"global rotation, support in the 8 site-2-base labels\", eqG8, GroebnerBasis[eqG8, {aa, bb, cc}], \"c^2 = b = 19a+9c = 0, i.e. a = b = c = 0: nothing\"},\n   {\"global rotation, support in the 3 Wronskian labels\", eqG3, GroebnerBasis[eqG3, {aa, bb, cc}], \"a = b = c = 0: nothing\"}};\nColumn[{\n  Grid[Prepend[genTab1, {\"generator (site-1-local)\", \"SoV label offsets {site1, site2}\", \"c_x with only that generator on\"}], Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left],\n  Grid[{{\"support of the general site-1-local rotation\", locSupport},\n     {\"labels with site 2 at base\", site1Block},\n     {\"is the site-1-local rotation supported there?\", SubsetQ[site1Block, locSupport]},\n     {\"the three labels of the 2x2 Wronskian minor\", {threeLab, labelOffset[patPairs[[#]]] & /@ threeLab}}}, Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left],\n  Grid[Prepend[Table[{r[[1]], Length[r[[2]]], r[[3]], r[[4]]}, {r, gbTab}],\n    {\"which rotations are supported inside the label set?\", \"#equations\", \"Groebner basis of the ideal\", \"conclusion\"}], Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left]}]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
      {
       "data": "<div class=\"wl-output-block\"><div class=\"wl-output-header\" style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:6px;width:100%;min-height:22px;\" data-session-epoch=\"24223\" data-output-id=\"20\" data-out-n=\"264\" data-sub-idx=\"16\" data-output-format=\"WLLatex\" data-output-is-graphics=\"0\" data-cell-idx=\"51\" data-sub-start=\"24\" data-sub-end=\"31\"><span style=\"font-size:10px;color:#888;margin-right:8px;\">Out[264]=</span></div><div class=\"wl-output-content\"><div class=\"vscode-wolfram-wllatex-prerendered\" data-page-width-em=\"21\" data-latex-b64=\"\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:43.3304em;vertical-align:-21.4152em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mtable\"><span class=\"col-align-c\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:21.9152em;\"><span style=\"top:-23.9152em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:17.2902em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:15.2902em;\"><span style=\"top:-17.2502em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:17.2502em;\"></span><span class=\"mtable\"><span class=\"vertical-separator\" style=\"height:30.0004em;border-right-width:0.04em;border-right-style:solid;margin:0 -0.02em;vertical-align:-14.7502em;\"></span><span class=\"arraycolsep\" style=\"width:0.5em;\"></span><span class=\"col-align-c\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:15.2502em;\"><span style=\"top:-17.8602em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.45em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">generator (site-1-local)</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-16.0502em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.45em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class