  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### Result: the product is *nearly* right — it fixes $1$, $X_1$, $X_2$ exactly and fails only in $X_1X_2$, by generating $X_1^2$\n\n**What the object is.** Fitting the product over all 24 states against the six monomials leaves\nresidual $0$ to **72 digits**, at every $(k,k')$ tried, so the object is *exactly*\n\n$$D(k,k')=G_c(X_1,X_2)\\,\\big(1+c'X_1\\big),\\qquad\nG_c=1+\\tfrac{9c}{28}X_1+\\tfrac{47c}{28}X_2+c^2X_1X_2,$$\n\nwith $c=-e^{kh}$ (the global parameter of §14) and $c'=-e^{k'h}/z_1$ (the local parameter of §8; the\n$z_1$ is there because that bracket is written in $q_1$ rather than $Q_1$). Measured: $c=-1,-1,-e^{1/2}$\nand $c'=-\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{e^{1/2}}{2},-\\tfrac{e^{-1}}{2}$ for $(k,k')=(0,0),(0,\\tfrac12),(\\tfrac12,-1)$.\n\n**What $\\langle0|g_1g_2$ is.** Since $E_{32}^{(1)},E_{32}^{(2)}$ commute,\n$g_1g_2=e^{(c+c')E_{32}}\\otimes e^{cE_{32}}$, whose exact SoV expansion (§16) is\n\n$$P=1+\\Big(\\tfrac{9c}{28}+c'\\Big)X_1+\\tfrac{47c}{28}X_2+c\\,(c+c')\\,X_1X_2 .$$\n\n**The difference, exactly:**\n\n$$D-P=\\frac{c\\,c'}{28}\\;X_1\\Big(9X_1+19X_2+28c\\,X_1X_2\\Big).$$\n\nSo the constant, the $X_1$ and the $X_2$ coefficients come out **exactly** right (all three vanish in\nthe difference to 72 digits) — the instinct is correct. What goes wrong is that the $X_1X_2$\ncoefficient overshoots by $\\tfrac{19}{28}cc'$, and two monomials appear that no covector on those\nlabels can have: $X_1^2$ and $X_1^2X_2$, with coefficients $\\tfrac{9}{28}cc'$ and $c^2c'$.\n\n**Why, structurally.** $X_1^2$ is not a wave-function ratio. The site-1 label only takes\n$x_{11}\\in\\{\\theta_1+h,\\theta_1+2h\\}$, so $\\Psi_A(x)/\\Psi_A(x_0)$ is of degree $\\le1$ in $X_1$. The\nproduct has $Q_1$ at a site-1 $x_{11}$ point **twice** — once from the $2\\times2$ determinant, once from\nthe one-site bracket — while $\\Psi_A$ carries only one $Q_1(x_{11})$ per site, and dividing by\n$Q_1(\\theta_1{+}h)^2Q_1(\\theta_2)$ cannot repair that: it is the right normalisation for the product,\nbut the product itself has one power of $Q_1$ too many at site 1.\n\nThe measured consequences on all 24 states confirm it: $\\mathrm{rank}\\{c(z),c(z')\\}=2$ (so not even an\nSoV expansion — the coefficients are twist-dependent), 14 of the 24 labels are used instead of 4,\nline-stabiliser dimension 1 instead of 6, and after fitting the best state-independent constant the\ndeviation from $\\langle0|g_1g_2|\\Psi_A\\rangle$ is $O(1)$ (2.4 to 13, depending on $k,k'$).\n\n**The minimal correction.** The identity that does hold, verified to be *identically* zero difference:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\;\\langle0|g_1g_2\\;\\longleftrightarrow\\;G_c+c'\\,X_1\\big(1+c\\,X_2\\big)\\;}$$\n\nIn words: the one-site dressing must multiply **only the site-2 leg** $(1+cX_2)$, not the whole global\nobject. Multiplying the whole object is exactly what produces the spurious $X_1^2$. Note $1+cX_2$ is\nitself a one-site bracket on site 2 at the *same* parameter $c$, so the correct object is\n\n$$\\text{global} \\;+\\; c'\\times(\\text{site-1 }x_{11}\\text{ shift})\\times(\\text{site-2 bracket at }c),$$\n\na **sum** of two products rather than a product — i.e. an antisymmetric combination, which is what a\ndeterminant produces naturally. That is precisely the object §19 builds: there the tuned insertion\n$g(u)$ is the device that generates the mixed term without ever putting two $Q_1$'s on the same site.\nSo per-site rotations with independent $(c_1,c_2)$ *are* determinants of $Q$-integrals — just not this\nproduct.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## 21. The corrected object as a single $2\\times2$ determinant, with the simplest insertion\n\n§20 gives the target in closed form, so we can now ask for the *simplest* determinant realising it.\nFrom §19, a $2\\times2$ determinant with per-site weight ratios $R,S$ and insertion $g$ produces\n\n$$c_{12}=S\\delta,\\qquad c_{21}=R(1-\\gamma),\\qquad c_{22}=RS(\\delta-\\gamma),$$\n\nwhere $\\gamma,\\delta$ are the values of $g$ at the two \"second\" points after the affine normalisation\n$g(p_{1,1})=0$, $g(p_{2,1})=1$ (the determinant is invariant under $g\\to\\alpha g+\\beta$, so this costs\nnothing). Eliminating $R$ and $S$ leaves **one** condition, and it involves only the insertion:\n\n$$\\frac{c_{22}}{c_{12}c_{21}}=\\frac{\\delta-\\gamma}{(1-\\gamma)\\,\\delta}\\;=:\\;K .$$\n\nFeeding in the per-site target $\\big(c_{12},c_{21},c_{22}\\big)=\\big(\\tfrac{47}{28}c_2,\\;c_1-\\tfrac{19}{28}c_2,\\;c_1c_2\\big)$ and solving for the ratio $\\rho=c_1/c_2$:\n\n$$\\rho=\\frac{893\\,K}{1316\\,K-784},\\qquad\\text{equivalently}\\qquad K=\\frac{784\\,\\rho}{1316\\,\\rho-893}.$$\n\nSo **the insertion fixes the ratio $c_1/c_2$, and the weights fix the scale.** Two immediate checks:\nthe linear insertion $g(u)=u$ gives $\\gamma=-\\tfrac{19}{28}$, $\\delta=\\tfrac{9}{28}$, hence\n$K=\\tfrac{784}{423}$ and $\\rho=1$ — the global rotation of §13, exactly as it must; and §16's product\nlocus $\\rho=\\tfrac{47}{28}$ must correspond to its own $K$.\n\nThis also explains §19's cubic: $\\gamma$ was chosen arbitrarily and the four values then needed a cubic\ninterpolant. Only $K$ matters, so we are free to pick the *shape* of $g$ and solve for its one\nparameter. Three natural one-parameter families, in increasing order of exoticism:\n\n1. **a single extra pole**, $g(u)=\\dfrac{1}{u-a}$ — the second column is the same bracket with one\n   extra pole in the measure;\n2. **an exponential**, $g(u)=e^{k''u}$ — the dressing that appears everywhere else in this notebook;\n3. **a quadratic polynomial** through the four points — one degree lower than §19's cubic.\n\nEach has exactly one parameter, matching the one condition $K$, so each should hit any target ratio.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* ---- 27. The simplest insertion realising a given per-site rotation, and its check on all 24 states. ---- *)\nClearAll[gv, gamOf, delOf, Kof, rhoOfK, KofRho, poleIns, expIns, quadIns, solvePole, solveExp, solveQuad,\n  RSof, psRunG, insTab, verTab, aa0, kk0, p11, p12, p21, p22];\n{p11, p12} = ptsSite[[1]]; {p21, p22} = ptsSite[[2]];\ngv[g_, x_] := g /. u -> x;\ngamOf[g_] := (gv[g, p12] - gv[g, p11])/(gv[g, p21] - gv[g, p11]);\ndelOf[g_] := (gv[g, p22] - gv[g, p11])/(gv[g, p21] - gv[g, p11]);\nKof[g_] := With[{ga = gamOf[g], de = delOf[g]}, (de - ga)/((1 - ga) de)];\nrhoOfK[K_] := 893 K/(1316 K - 784);\nKofRho[rho_] := 784 rho/(1316 rho - 893);\n(* the three one-parameter insertion families *)\npoleIns[a_] := 1/(u - a);\nexpIns[kk_] := Exp[kk u];\nquadIns[a_] := (u - a)^2;\nsolvePole[rho_] := With[{s = Solve[Kof[poleIns[aa0]] == KofRho[rho], aa0]}, If[s === {}, Missing[], aa0 /. First[s]]];\nsolveQuad[rho_] := With[{s = Solve[Kof[quadIns[aa0]] == KofRho[rho], aa0]}, If[s === {}, Missing[], aa0 /. First[s]]];\nsolveExp[rho_] := Quiet @ Check[kk0 /. FindRoot[Kof[expIns[kk0]] == N[KofRho[rho], 40], {kk0, 1/3}, WorkingPrecision -> 40], Missing[]];\n(* weights for a given target and insertion *)\nRSof[c1_, c2_, g_] := With[{ga = gamOf[g], de = delOf[g]}, {(c1 - (19/28) c2)/(1 - ga), ((47/28) c2)/de}];\n(* run the object on all 24 states *)\npsRunG[label_, c1_, c2_, g_] := Module[{RS = RSof[c1, c2, g], cons, cs, V, Vn, mat, sv, f1, f2},\n   cons = Abs[RS[[1]] RS[[2]] (delOf[g] - gamOf[g]) - c1 c2]/Abs[c1 c2];\n   cs = Table[With[{ch = chainM[zz]}, LinearSolve[ch[\"P\"], Table[detFree2[st[\"q\"], zz, N[RS, wpS], g], {st, ch[\"states\"]}]]], {zz, {zX, zP}}];\n   V = cs[[1]] . chainM[zX][\"xNorm\"]; Vn = V/V[[First @ Ordering[-Abs[V], 1]]];\n   mat = Partition[Vn, d2]; sv = SingularValueList[mat, Tolerance -> 0];\n   f1 = fitRep[mat[[All, d2]], λ1]; f2 = fitRep[mat[[d1]], λ2];\n   {label, N[{c1, c2}, 6], sci[cons], numRank[cs, 10^-40], sci[sv[[2]]/sv[[1]]],\n    {N[f1[[1, 3]], 8], N[f2[[1, 3]], 8]}, sci[Max[Abs[f1[[2]]], Abs[f2[[2]]]]]}];\n\n(* sanity: which ratio does each familiar insertion give? *)\ninsTab = {\n   {\"g = u (section 13)\", gamOf[u], delOf[u], Kof[u], Simplify[rhoOfK[Kof[u]]]},\n   {\"g = u^2\", gamOf[u^2], delOf[u^2], Kof[u^2], Simplify[rhoOfK[Kof[u^2]]]},\n   {\"g = 1/u\", gamOf[1/u], delOf[1/u], Kof[1/u], Simplify[rhoOfK[Kof[1/u]]]}};\n\n(* Paul's target: c = -e^{kh} (global), c' = -e^{k'h}/z1 (local), (c1,c2) = (c+c', c) *)\nWith[{k = 0, kp = 0}, cPaul = -Exp[k hX]; cpPaul = -Exp[kp hX]/zX[[1]]];\nrhoPaul = (cPaul + cpPaul)/cPaul;\naPole = solvePole[rhoPaul]; aQuad = solveQuad[rhoPaul]; kExp = solveExp[rhoPaul];\nverTab = {\n   psRunG[\"single pole 1/(u-a)\", cPaul + cpPaul, cPaul, poleIns[aPole]],\n   psRunG[\"quadratic (u-a)^2\", cPaul + cpPaul, cPaul, quadIns[aQuad]],\n   psRunG[\"exponential e^{k'' u}\", cPaul + cpPaul, cPaul, expIns[kExp]],\n   psRunG[\"control: g = u (wrong ratio)\", cPaul + cpPaul, cPaul, u]};\nColumn[{\n  Grid[Prepend[insTab, {\"insertion\", \"gamma\", \"delta\", \"K\", \"forced ratio c1/c2\"}], Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left],\n  Grid[{{\"Paul's c (global), c' (local), target ratio rho\", {N[cPaul, 8], N[cpPaul, 8], N[rhoPaul, 8]}},\n     {\"required K\", {KofRho[rhoPaul], N[KofRho[rhoPaul], 10]}},\n     {\"single pole: a\", {aPole, N[aPole, 10]}},\n     {\"quadratic: a\", {aQuad, N[aQuad, 10]}},\n     {\"exponential: k''\", N[kExp, 10]}}, Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left],\n  Grid[Prepend[verTab, {\"insertion\", \"target (c1,c2)\", \"consistency residual\", \"rank{c(z),c(z')}\",\n     \"sigma2/sigma1\", \"achieved (c1,c2)\", \"per-site fit residual\"}], Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left]}]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ],
 "metadata": {
